{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1529745-mage-the-ascention-as-personal-horror-game","id":1529745,"title":"Mage the Ascention as Personal Horror game.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-01-31T02:42:52-08:00","id":1529745,"title":"Mage the Ascention as Personal Horror game.","content":"Hey I'm trying to construct a campaign that will be more in line atmospherically with other splats like Vampire, Wraith, Werewolf or Demon in that Mages will be &quot;The monsters&quot;.<br /> Currently Mage is a Horror about 1984 totalitarian state or about well-meaning but flawed people or maybe about dangerous and unforeseen consequences of using magic.<br /> The other splats draw the PERSONAL-horror from making the player a monster himself. I'd like mages to be a little bit scary.<br /> Every splat has some kind of &quot;Beast&quot;.<br /> <br /> Vampires have hunger for blood, immortality and &quot;The Beast&quot; that not only slowly chips away their humanity, but also makes them wild predators.<br /> In werewolf from what I understand &quot;The Beast&quot; is the inner Rage that is difficult to control and their mission, tribe and conviction makes the Rage &quot;justifiable&quot; but tragic. (I didn't play it, so sorry if that is off the mark.)<br /> In Demon you have torment from eons past inflicted by your family on you. It is hate and rebellious ire for the Creator Himself which in turn spoils your very essence.<br /> In Wraith you have a dark half of yourself that turns your passions and character into some kind of abomination.<br /> <br /> In all of those cases it is undeniable that you are the monster. Killing, controlling, self-righteous monster and it is easy to be one because you are compelled by your supernatural nature.<br /> With mage it is a lot more difficult. Even if a mage has huge flaws they mostly are well meaning. The whole setting is centered around fighting for freedom or fighting monsters. (Nephandi, marouders)<br /> Mages do have huge power but that does not make them automatically pricks, which is very much the case in other splats. If I made mages pricks without their very nature compelling them to I'd feel like there would be something wrong with them even without powers. It feels unrealistic to make most of them or all of them corrupt because they have magic. It's not in the same league of compulsion as &quot;The Beast&quot;.<br /> <br /> I had this idea that I will make the Avatar the compulsion of Mage. The immortal otherworldly spirit that has it's own goals. It is mage's higher self in a sense so it will push him towards personal ascension but will have no regard for anything that is outside it's personal &quot;paradigm&quot;. It itself believes that world works in some certain mystic way and it acts like it's true. It also might imprint those principles on the world.<br /> With that kind of compulsion I can easily justify why mages do monstrous stuff without making it look like I have a cringe'y cynical views on human nature and morality.<br /> <br /> Any other ideas ? Do you have a proposition for this Avatar-compulsion system ?<br /> Do you make your Mages monstrous ? How do you achieve that ?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28345,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28345-sirresnick","user":"SirResnick","date":". 01-31-2024, 05:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28345-sirresnick","name":"SirResnick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-31T04:02:10-08:00","id":1529747,"title":"","content":"The key to personal horror in Mage for me is remembering that the road to hell is paved with good intentions. Mages are monsters because they have ridiculous amounts of power and almost zero reasons not to use it to maximum effect. What's worse, they can bend reality, including morality, to make their actions just. Mages are at their worst when their acting like any human would if granted reality warping superpowers. <br /> <br /> I really don't like the idea that you need a &quot;Beast&quot; or equivalent thereof. Rage in Werewolf is not the same as a vampire's Beast, a wraith's Shadow, or a demon's Torment. Each is unique and different as tailored to the themes of those games. Changelings, hunters, mummies, and Orpheus agents have plenty of horror to deal with without such codified issues to content with as well.<br /> <br /> If you really want to add something, I would steal from Mage the Awakening 2e: each mage in that version of the game is irrevocably obsessed in an unhealthy way. They have to poke a little more, prod a little harder, bend the rules just a bit more, to sate whatever their current desire is to sate their obsessions. This keeps to their very human monstrosity, by focusing on keeping their motives personal and relatable, but always tainted by the fact that their power feels like it's meant to be used, and there's always something to use it to do; even if they shouldn't from the audience perspective.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-31T05:21:59-08:00","id":1529750,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1529745-mage-the-ascention-as-personal-horror-game?p=1529747#post1529747\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The key to personal horror in Mage for me is remembering that the road to hell is paved with good intentions. Mages are monsters because they have ridiculous amounts of power and almost zero reasons not to use it to maximum effect. What's worse, they can bend reality, including morality, to make their actions just. Mages are at their worst when their acting like any human would if granted reality warping superpowers.<br /> perspective.</div> </div> </div> </div>You mean like monkey paw logic ? It often works, but takes ages to actually develop with PCs. On the day-to-day basis being a mage is not too horrifying. Unless you factor in the Big Brother and paranoia that goes with it. I don't want to authoritatively say one thing or the other, so I'll try to convey what I feel.<br /> When I play mage I feel grim gothic atmosphere around me but hopeful and powerful inside me (Which is the opposite of horror)<br /> Then when I play Vampire or Demon I feel that grimness inside me and I can feel the creeping evil that corrupts my actions and thoughts.<br /> <br /> So it is not necessarily bad to be mage, but it just so happens that it often has unforeseen fuck-ups. Which is unsettling when you do it a few times, but to me the theme doesn't hold the atmosphere for long as it is by nature &quot;the conclusion of actions&quot; not the action itself. So the majority of session there is little to be unsettled about, because at heart mages are well meaning to humanity with a few exceptions.<br /> Unlike in Vampire where killing is in the Vampire's nature and reinforced by the kindred community. So you do it and think about it on a daily basis.<br /> That is why I want to give mage community a reason to be horrific not merely stumble over their own power.<br /> <br /> The hubris is a good measure system, but it doesn't feel organic if you assume that this hubris stems only from their humanity.<br /> I think I just don't believe that most people with reality warping superpowers would be automatically possessed by hubris to the point of being inhuman. I do believe that they will often fuck-up in a major way, but that does not make them monsters in my eyes. They would be tragic heroes at best.<br /> I feel like someone is a monster when I see him as completely/partially inhuman and devoid of some or all human qualities like basic empathy.<br /> For example psychopaths are monstrous in my mind despite being Homo Sapiens.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28345,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28345-sirresnick","user":"SirResnick","date":". 01-31-2024, 08:27 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28345-sirresnick","name":"SirResnick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-31T06:42:45-08:00","id":1529751,"title":"","content":"To develop what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> said, the biggest source of personal horror comes from the fact that mages are human and humans are monsters tempered only by their lack of power and the consequences of their actions. Mages, given power and shielded from the consequences of their actions, are primed to become monsters without ever realizing it.<br /> <br /> As you say, though, there's not really a system to bring that into focus. It's easy to see what horrors other mages do, but not the players.<br /> <br /> The Avatar, as an otherworldly force in a Mage's life works, but unless you shift focus so that all Avatars are working towards alien and ultimately horrible goals they're going to be a push towards strangeness, not horror.<br /> <br /> Instead, the system I would tweak for personal horror is Paradox. Paradox is already set up to be a consequence of a mage's actions and one of the suggestions for how to handle it is to consider it a reflection of the fears or motivations of the characters. So focus on that. Tweak Paradox so that it has as much of a caustic effect on local reality as it does on the Mage or so that they can choose between taking a backlash or letting it permeate the world like toxic waste. Make backlashes a reflection of the mage's goals. Give them what they want but sometimes, and only sometimes, in the way they least want it. That might get you the personal horror element you want.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-31T07:36:26-08:00","id":1529755,"title":"","content":"What you're looking for is Quiet. That's the Mage equivalent of frenzy in Vampire, death-rage in Werewolf, being taken over by your Shadow in Wraith, and going monstrous in Demon.<br /> <br /> The concept might be revised or expanded on: for instance, you might more rigorously define how and when one enters Quiet; and you might revise the state of Quiet to take away a bit more player agency. After all, that's the whole point of the various systems you pointed to in the other game, to force the character to act in monstrous ways, whether the player wants him to or not. <br /> <br /> I might consider revising Morbidity to something more general \u2014 something that's not <i>necessarily</i> about death in particular, but rather about some primordial element of reality. Death and decay is just one possibility among many; but the idea is that whatever the primordial concept that you're drawn to, you tend to become more like it the deeper your Quiet becomes, and the more that concept manifests around you. The Marauder equivalent would be someone who has <i>become </i>a manifestation of the force of nature in question: a bit like the Exemplars from <b>Masters of the Art</b>, but not tied specifically to Spheres (unless you <i>want</i> to do that) and reconceptualized to apply to regular mages instead of being an Archmaster thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-31T07:51:03-08:00","id":1529756,"title":"","content":"Note that I don't disagree with what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> and <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar758_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"758\" data-vbnamecard=\"758\">Ramnesis</a> are saying; but to me, that's not so much \u201cpersonal horror\u201d as it is \u201csocial commentary\u201d. Which doesn't make it any less impactful; mind you. And my points about Quiet should be considered in that context: Quiet is best used to enhance the \u201chuman nature combined with godlike power is a recipe for disaster\u201d notion.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-31T08:29:34-08:00","id":1529757,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1529745-mage-the-ascention-as-personal-horror-game?p=1529755#post1529755\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What you're looking for is Quiet. That's the Mage equivalent of frenzy in Vampire, <b>death-rage in Werewolf</b>, being taken over by your Shadow in Wraith, and going monstrous in Demon.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you may have crossed some wires there. In W:tA, it's frenzy, but also not personal horror, as you might need it to avoid being splattered in turn from the splatter-horror milieu.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-31T09:58:01-08:00","id":1529760,"title":"","content":"It's actually not <i>my</i> point; it's what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28345-sirresnick\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"28345\" data-vbnamecard=\"28345\">SirResnick</a> was saying. I just didn't want to get too deep into the weeds on how they different games feature different kinds of horror.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-31T13:23:53-08:00","id":1529767,"title":"","content":"Some stuff that might be useful are these old archived pages from Anders Mage Page about using horror and strangeness in mage storytelling:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://mage.gearsonline.net/anders/magick/philosophy/horror-enlighten.html\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"http://mage.gearsonline.net/anders/img/title01.png\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://mage.gearsonline.net/anders/magick/philosophy/horror-enlighten.html\" target=\"_blank\"> Horror of Enlightenment - Anders Mage Page 2.0 </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://mage.gearsonline.net/anders/magick/philosophy/horror-enlighten.html\" target=\"_blank\"> http://mage.gearsonline.net/anders/magick/philosophy/horror-enlighten.html </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://mage.gearsonline.net/anders/paradox/about/weirdness.html\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"http://mage.gearsonline.net/anders/img/title01.png\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://mage.gearsonline.net/anders/paradox/about/weirdness.html\" target=\"_blank\"> Daily Weirdness - Anders Mage Page 2.0 </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://mage.gearsonline.net/anders/paradox/about/weirdness.html\" target=\"_blank\"> http://mage.gearsonline.net/anders/paradox/about/weirdness.html </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> (most of the stuff on that site was designed for 1e/2e but the more high concept stuff can still be interesting and useful)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-31T22:25:33-08:00","id":1529803,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SirResnick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1529745-mage-the-ascention-as-personal-horror-game?p=1529750#post1529750\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You mean like monkey paw logic ? It often works, but takes ages to actually develop with PCs. On the day-to-day basis being a mage is not too horrifying. Unless you factor in the Big Brother and paranoia that goes with it. I don't want to authoritatively say one thing or the other, so I'll try to convey what I feel.<br /> When I play mage I feel grim gothic atmosphere around me but hopeful and powerful inside me (Which is the opposite of horror)<br /> Then when I play Vampire or Demon I feel that grimness inside me and I can feel the creeping evil that corrupts my actions and thoughts.<br /> <br /> So it is not necessarily bad to be mage, but it just so happens that it often has unforeseen fuck-ups. Which is unsettling when you do it a few times, but to me the theme doesn't hold the atmosphere for long as it is by nature &quot;the conclusion of actions&quot; not the action itself. So the majority of session there is little to be unsettled about, because at heart mages are well meaning to humanity with a few exceptions.<br /> Unlike in Vampire where killing is in the Vampire's nature and reinforced by the kindred community. So you do it and think about it on a daily basis.<br /> That is why I want to give mage community a reason to be horrific not merely stumble over their own power.<br /> <br /> The hubris is a good measure system, but it doesn't feel organic if you assume that this hubris stems only from their humanity.<br /> I think I just don't believe that most people with reality warping superpowers would be automatically possessed by hubris to the point of being inhuman. I do believe that they will often fuck-up in a major way, but that does not make them monsters in my eyes. They would be tragic heroes at best.<br /> I feel like someone is a monster when I see him as completely/partially inhuman and devoid of some or all human qualities like basic empathy.<br /> For example psychopaths are monstrous in my mind despite being Homo Sapiens.</div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;Monkey Paw logic&quot; is the desired outcome being 'twisted' in some fashion that makes it bad when it otherwise should have been good.<br /> <br /> What <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> is getting at is a bit more subtle: the idea of getting what you want, and not realizing that, over time, &quot;what you want&quot; has extended far beyond your original expectations, and out of the bounds of decency or morality. You are 100% correct that it is harder to set up, and takes a lot longer. And that is a consequence of how Mage was designed in comparison to the other game-lines.<br /> <br /> Part of that is that things like Vampires and Werewolves, in pop culture, have obvious and explicit &quot;monstrosity.&quot; They are The Other in ways that are obvious, fundamental, and dangerous. The pop-culture notion of a &quot;mage/wizard/etc.&quot; just doesn't have a lot of that hardwired into it. Sure, we have evil witches, demon-summoners, and such, but the concept of Magic itself being evil and dangerous is mostly found within viewpoints defined by religious extremism, at least in my experience. Magic, like science, tends to default to &quot;ideologically neutral&quot; in fiction. Magic is less evil in itself, and more a means of enabling evil (or good).<br /> <br /> Therefore, the monstrosity in Mage has to come from the choices that the Mage makes in how to use their power. You can be heavy-handed with stuff like Nephandi and Marauders, but horror with &quot;regular&quot; Mages pretty much has to be subtle.<br /> <br /> I do think that there is one fundamental problem with your assessment:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So the majority of session there is little to be unsettled about, because at heart mages are well meaning to humanity with a few exceptions. </div> </div> </div>This is largely wrong, or at least missing a mountain of nuance. The &quot;average&quot; Mage cares as much about humanity as the average Joe. Which is how we end up with an Orwellian conspiracy manipulating humanity &quot;for our own good&quot; as one of the major factions in Mage, one that quickly joined the ranks of playable characters as soon as people started to push back against the one-dimensional portrayal of said faction. If you really want to find the low-hanging fruit for Horror in Mage, you just need to start exploring the implications of Power when it is combined with the idea that the people with Power are convinced that they are using it for the &quot;Greater Good.&quot; The Traditions have absolutely the same amount of potential for personal horror as the abuses of the Technocracy. You just need to find the situation where they would justify something &quot;monstrous&quot; by our standards which fits *their* conception of the &quot;Greater Good.&quot;<br /> <br /> This is really easy to apply to NPCs. The Traditions are overflowing with opportunities for their ideals to be twisted, abused, or even just applied in a way that can mess with the norms and mores established by our society. Everyone's favourite example in the Traditions is the Chakravanti/Euthanatos. You know, the folks who will sometimes kill a person with no explanation other than &quot;Karma said so.&quot; If you dig a bit more into the Sahajiya/Cult of Ecstasy, you're going to find that there's a split between the folks who believe that &quot;opening someone's mind&quot; should only be done with (somewhat) informed consent, and the ones who are willing to mind-rape someone for &quot;their own good.&quot; There is a ton of potential for monstrous behaviour in Mages.<br /> <br /> The tricky part is dragging this horror into the realm of the Player Characters. Part of this has to do with the extensive planning involved. But the other part has to do with the Players, and their intentions. Among more nuanced and experienced roleplayers, you can find folks who are interested in exploring moral complexities and unusual beliefs, but with many people you will find them reductively wanting to be either &quot;good&quot; or &quot;bad.&quot; If your Players want to be Good, then the Players will mostly avoid moral complexity in favour of their own personal conception of 'goodness' and will refuse to participate in anything that obviously conflicts with the moral code they have decided to follow. If your Players want to be &quot;bad,&quot; then you're going to have a very hard time managing to create the crisis involved in Personal Horror because they have committed to monstrosity and are probably not going to be spooked out of it unless you go out of your way to invoke genuine feelings of Yuck.<br /> <br /> Creating a Personal Horror situation for a Vampire or Werewolf is easy, because they have powerful impulses that are threatening to conventional human existence. Creating a Personal Horror situation with Mage is more difficult because you can't lean on any built-in sources of horror: you have to create the horror yourself. My favourite go-to is the Mind Sphere, because I used to be the kind of person who didn't understand the horror of devastating the freedom and identity of another sentient being as a method of avoiding something we consider more obviously evil like murder. It's easy enough to look at something like the Jedi Mind Trick in Episode VI of <u>Star Wars</u> and think of it as relatively innocent, just sneaking past a security checkpoint. But then look at Episode II where Obi-Wan brainwashes someone so that they &quot;go home and rethink their life.&quot; Sure, that's arguably for their benefit in some sense, but that's also a fundamental violation of their personal autonomy: sure, telling someone to think really hard about their decision to become a drug-dealer is a good thing to do, but when you literally FORCE THEM to reconsider that choice, it is a violation of their autonomy regardless of whether or not they are happier with the outcome.<br /> <br /> So, if you want to bring Personal Horror to Mage without necessarily hardwiring it into the mechanics, you need to design your stories in such a way that your characters are forced to question &quot;the Greater Good&quot; that they are (presumably) trying to serve (if you are trying to tell a story about Personal Horror: again, it's really hard to do this if your Players are just committed to being assholes). You need to create situations where your Players will be tempted to overlook idealistic principles for pragmatism, and keep slowly building that up, until you get them to the point where you can suddenly Wham them with the fact that they are (again, using the Mind Sphere as an example) using their powers to manipulate everyone around them &quot;for their own good,&quot; with bonus points if you can slap them in the face with how the stuff they believed was for the right reasons was actually just serving their own agenda.<br /> <br /> That kind of stuff is <b>complicated</b>. <br /> <br /> Mage just isn't built for the kind of horror that you find in Vampire or Werewolf. It's the kind of horror that you find in the story &quot;The Ones Who Walk Away from Omelas&quot; by Ursula K. Le Guin. You can't really introduce horror into the setup of Mage without being willing to explore it in a subtle and cumulative fashion, or you have to do more to hardwire it into the mechanics. And the former option is extremely difficult if you don't want to make any kind of &quot;authoritative&quot; statement on the values involved in the discussion. I haven't had the opportunity to use it before now, but one of the expressions that I've come up with when imagining how to explain Mage to people who haven't been exposed before is &quot;(full-contact) philosophical combat.&quot; You can't really add more horror to what's already there without being able to decide <b>for yourself</b> what &quot;horror&quot; means.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25886-kharnov","name":"Kharnov","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"683"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-01T00:23:16-08:00","id":1529806,"title":"","content":"The problem with &quot;personal horror&quot; in general is that regardless of mechanics, players really have to buy into the concept for it to work. Lots of people play dark super heroes with fangs/Katanas and Trenchcoats with VtM and don't give a shit about the personal horror side of the game.<br /> <br /> If your players buy into personal horror and mage, they simply need to buy into the ideas that (a) humans aren't inherently morally good, and (b) power shows you for you really are.<br /> <br /> Magic is the ultimate temptation. It's the allegorical ring of invisibility. Magic is, at its core in Mage, cheating. Once you start justifying the small cheats, you're on the road to justifying more and more cheating. You have your personal codes and morals that say some kinds of cheating are bad, but others are acceptable, but that acceptable sandbox is always going to be more than permissive enough to be horrible.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-01T11:13:53-08:00","id":1529828,"title":"","content":"<br /> <br /> Wow - thank you for that post. it's extensive and helpful in it's own way, but I'm not sure we understand each other 100% so let me ask/clarify a few things.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kharnov</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1529745-mage-the-ascention-as-personal-horror-game?p=1529803#post1529803\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> The &quot;average&quot; Mage cares as much about humanity as the average Joe.</div> </div> </div> </div>1. I'd say it's not negligible percent of people. I even checked it somewhat here : <a href=\"https://orbmedia.org/generation-activist-data\" target=\"_blank\">https://orbmedia.org/generation-activist-data</a><br /> It's not entirely clear but I'd average the percentage of activists to 18.5% (Assuming 1/4 population is young and 3/4 is old )<br /> That is only people who actually go on protests and other informal political actions. If you factor people who just care about their town or simply very much care about moral formation (Like in religious groups), then I'd say we will easily go up to 33%. <br /> Apart from that my personal opinion about people and the nature of humanity is that people actually do very much care about humanity at large in one way or another. Those who actually don't care at all are often &quot;broken&quot;.<br /> The question I ask myself then is &quot;How a normal person like that changes into true monster ?&quot; and to be honest my only actual answer is &quot;Outside influence&quot; be it avatar, paradox or life experience some people become sociopaths. However it feels like too little of a chance to paint mage society as inherently broken or monstrous. And I feel like I need monstrous NPCs around to keep the atmosphere gothic-eearie. (I don't want to just assume that everybody is a brooding A-hole despite I know that it is kind of WoD cinematographic assumption - I just can't suspend my disbelief.) <br /> If most mages are monsters it also creates &quot;trying to escape the inevitable&quot; theme in the story, which I like. However I feel like I need a good reason to why most mages do monstrous stuff daily and I'm not sold on the &quot;Power corrupts&quot; idea. It certainly does, but rarely to the point of monstrosity and I need it to be common thing. <br /> <br /> To be clear let me reiterate on what I mean by monster : &quot;when I see him as completely/partially inhuman and devoid of some or all human qualities like basic empathy.&quot;<br /> So a mage that steered someones mind clearly for the benefit of that person isn't necessarily a monster because the problem is complex and the answer depends on how do you define freedom and how much of a big deal it is in your philosophy. <br /> He would be a monster if he truly lost regard for other people OR His notion of &quot;Greater good&quot; is so far removed from conventional morality that it seems villanous to almost all people (Which means it must overtly violate some basic right like the right to live)<br /> If that is not the case and the character actually just struggles philosophically, then he is not a monster. He is a tragic hero. There is nothing monstrous about having philosophical breakdowns. It is scary, but not monstrous. (Light Yagami from Death Note is firmly on the monster side but not because he kills criminals, but because he doesn't care about human life too much, That is why he ends up building a death cult.)<br /> <br /> 2. It is possible to descend into monstrosity by slowly abusing more and more of your power however as I said - It is mid to end game horror. In the early game PCs are relatively pure and usually have little to no intention to violate other people. Which makes the early game devoid of the uneasiness that is present in Vampire. I'm looking for something constant, some kind of looming presence over PCs that eventually makes them crack.<br /> On that note <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar421_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"421\" data-vbnamecard=\"421\">Dataweaver</a> I'm warming up to quiet idea, but I' t sure if making it effectively the effect of psychosis instead of some part of their very nature won't make the situation look like it's not entirely their fault. Hunger and Anger are more disturbing because it feels like it's YOU, like it's your very essence that did the deed. Psychosis comes and goes and it feels more like a brain-fart.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kharnov</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1529745-mage-the-ascention-as-personal-horror-game?p=1529803#post1529803\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Mage just isn't built for the kind of horror that you find in Vampire or Werewolf. It's the kind of horror that you find in the story &quot;The Ones Who Walk Away from Omelas&quot; by Ursula K. Le Guin. You can't really introduce horror into the setup of Mage without being willing to explore it in a subtle and cumulative fashion, or you have to do more to hardwire it into the mechanics. And the former option is extremely difficult if you don't want to make any kind of &quot;authoritative&quot; statement on the values involved in the discussion. I haven't had the opportunity to use it before now, but one of the expressions that I've come up with when imagining how to explain Mage to people who haven't been exposed before is &quot;(full-contact) philosophical combat.&quot; You can't really add more horror to what's already there without being able to decide <b>for yourself</b> what &quot;horror&quot; means</div> </div> </div> </div>Understood - However I have yet to meet a GM that can actually pull this off reliably. Especially with certain people who cannot be unsettled by that kind of horror too much because they either don't understand what they did wrong or understand the predicament so damn well that they learned to accept the outcomes it brings. <br /> It also makes for relatively non-horror early game which is totally killing the atmosphere for some people (For example to me.)<br /> However worst case scenario I can resign from the inner-horror in early game and just make it dark on the outside until the revelation strikes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28345-sirresnick","name":"SirResnick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-01T16:06:05-08:00","id":1529837,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SirResnick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1529745-mage-the-ascention-as-personal-horror-game?p=1529828#post1529828\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On that note <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver\">Dataweaver</a> I'm warming up to quiet idea, but I' t sure if making it effectively the effect of psychosis instead of some part of their very nature won't make the situation look like it's not entirely their fault. Hunger and Anger are more disturbing because it feels like it's YOU, like it's your very essence that did the deed. Psychosis comes and goes and it feels more like a brain-fart.</div> </div> </div> </div>If you review the write-ups of what the various levels of Quiet mean, you'll notice that the lowest levels, of Denial at least, aren't any kind of psychosis. Denial 1 manifests as stubbornness and mild projection (attributing your own faults to others); level 2 Denial involves selective perceptions (a.k.a., only seeing what you want to see; at this stage, figuratively) and hypocritical behavior. I'd go so far as to remove the Rose-colored Mirrorshades Flaw, which was introduced before <s>Clarity</s>Denial was added to the game, and replace it with a dot or two of a suitable Denial. It isn't until level 3 Denial that it enters the realm of psychotic behavior (irrational behavior, and<i> literal</i> blindness to denied subjects). Someone could easily walk around for years with Denial 1 and be perceived by others merely as an opinionated jerk. Madness becomes problematic much earlier, with even Madness 1 featuring minor hallucinations \u2014 though I personally play that less as hallucinations and more as delusions \u2014 believing things that are generally accepted to be false, as opposed to Denial which starts with denying things that are generally accepted to be true. If you shift it around that way, the first two levels of Madness aren't particularly psychotic, either. And Morbidity; well, like I said before, I think that's a bit too one-note for Mage as written. As I'd rewrite and expand it, the first couple of levels would involve something closer to an obsession with a subject.<br /> <br /> This is appropriate, considering that Quiet operates on a 5-point scale. The first dot should be subtle; the second, noticable; the third, strong; the fourth, <i>very</i> strong; and the fifth, overwhelming. Go beyond 5, and you become a Marauder. <br /> <br /> The other reason to broaden Morbidity would be to bring it in line with the other two: both Denial and Madness implicitly require you to pick a subject. With Madness, it's what sorts of delusions you have; with Denial, it's what you reject. And with the broadened replacement for Morbidity, it's what you obsess over. <br /> <hr /><br /> <br /> But since you mentioned psychosis, let's look that up: <i>\u201cPsychosis refers to a collection of symptoms that affect the mind, where there has been some loss of contact with reality.\u201d</i> \u2014 National Institute for Mental Health. \u201cSome loss of contact with reality\u201d strikes me as something <i>very </i>appropriate for a game where the protagonists figuratively fight against reality whenever they use their powers: \u201cI reject your reality and substitute my own.\u201d Conceptually, I would argue that psychosis could be to Mage as Hunger is to Vampire and Anger is to Werewolf. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-02T18:45:46-08:00","id":1529876,"title":"","content":"&quot;Mage As Personal Horror&quot;<br /> <br /> Wasn't that the whole intention behind Mage Revised? At least in the early part of it?<br /> <br /> That could be a potential source to tap into for ideas","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm just a trans guy and gaming fan, unabashed hedonist and unusually upbeat nihilist, fairly laid back. He/Him.<br /> <br /> If everything is political, then nothing is!","edited":{"userid":25920,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25920-matt-corleone","user":"Matt Corleone","date":". 02-02-2024, 09:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25920-matt-corleone","name":"Matt Corleone","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25920_8.webp","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"393"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-03T03:13:11-08:00","id":1529891,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Matt Corleone</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1529745-mage-the-ascention-as-personal-horror-game?p=1529876#post1529876\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wasn't that the whole intention behind Mage Revised? At least in the early part of it?</div> </div> </div> </div>The idea was to make the game more <i>personal</i>, so the game is about newer mages doing stuff on Earth, without much of the oversight of the Traditions. The horror elements are kinda the same as they were previously.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-04T19:11:05-08:00","id":1530000,"title":"","content":"I see <i>an</i> element of Personal Horror that arises from what Phil Brucato called &quot;The Fascist Urge&quot;. (Not a great name, since Fascism has its own loose collection of traits that aren't really this, but it's the name Brucato gave us).<br /> <br /> Simply put, that when you have the power to effect change on the world, there's always the temptation to think you're the only one qualified to make decisions for everyone else. That you know better, and that gives you license to impose your Will on everyone else. This form of what Robert L Moore called &quot;Grandiosity&quot; occurs in everyone - we all think we could solve every problem, if people would simply obey us. But it's especially active and dangerous in Mages, for whom Reality itself is something to be cut into the shape of their liking.<br /> <br /> In many ways, the &quot;Fascist Urge&quot; plays as much on a Mage's noble intentions as it does on their selfishness or malice. Indeed, a Mage with good intentions is in some ways MORE dangerous, as they genuinely believe themselves righteous and justified. Their Will is pure, you see, and so it would be wrong NOT to manifest that Will on the world and everyone in it, consent be damned. At least a craven monster is on some level aware their worst motives and impulses need to be hidden, as the masses consider them abhorrent. And because it's fairly easy to gratify the most base desires while having a comparatively minor impact on the larger setting. A Mage who believes they're righteous is not satisfied until the whole of their &quot;ideal world&quot; is made manifest, no matter what or who gets in their way.<br /> <br /> It's the Personal Horror of being made to even interrogate one's &quot;good intentions&quot;, and how presumption of rightness can, itself, lead to dark paths. This need not even be a theme that has anything to do with Magick or any inherent aspect of the Mage experience. It's a very human matter. All the Magick does is crank the Hubris and the potential damage to 11.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude00","count":1},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-04T20:41:23-08:00","id":1530003,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SirResnick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1529745-mage-the-ascention-as-personal-horror-game?p=1529828#post1529828\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wow - thank you for that post. it's extensive and helpful in it's own way, but I'm not sure we understand each other 100% so let me ask/clarify a few things.<br /> <br /> <br /> 1. I'd say it's not negligible percent of people. I even checked it somewhat here : <a href=\"https://orbmedia.org/generation-activist-data\" target=\"_blank\">https://orbmedia.org/generation-activist-data</a><br /> It's not entirely clear but I'd average the percentage of activists to 18.5% (Assuming 1/4 population is young and 3/4 is old )<br /> That is only people who actually go on protests and other informal political actions. If you factor people who just care about their town or simply very much care about moral formation (Like in religious groups), then I'd say we will easily go up to 33%.<br /> Apart from that my personal opinion about people and the nature of humanity is that people actually do very much care about humanity at large in one way or another. Those who actually don't care at all are often &quot;broken&quot;.<br /> The question I ask myself then is &quot;How a normal person like that changes into true monster ?&quot; and to be honest my only actual answer is &quot;Outside influence&quot; be it avatar, paradox or life experience some people become sociopaths. However it feels like too little of a chance to paint mage society as inherently broken or monstrous. And I feel like I need monstrous NPCs around to keep the atmosphere gothic-eearie. (I don't want to just assume that everybody is a brooding A-hole despite I know that it is kind of WoD cinematographic assumption - I just can't suspend my disbelief.)<br /> If most mages are monsters it also creates &quot;trying to escape the inevitable&quot; theme in the story, which I like. However I feel like I need a good reason to why most mages do monstrous stuff daily and I'm not sold on the &quot;Power corrupts&quot; idea. It certainly does, but rarely to the point of monstrosity and I need it to be common thing.<br /> <br /> To be clear let me reiterate on what I mean by monster : &quot;when I see him as completely/partially inhuman and devoid of some or all human qualities like basic empathy.&quot;<br /> So a mage that steered someones mind clearly for the benefit of that person isn't necessarily a monster because the problem is complex and the answer depends on how do you define freedom and how much of a big deal it is in your philosophy.<br /> He would be a monster if he truly lost regard for other people OR His notion of &quot;Greater good&quot; is so far removed from conventional morality that it seems villanous to almost all people (Which means it must overtly violate some basic right like the right to live)<br /> If that is not the case and the character actually just struggles philosophically, then he is not a monster. He is a tragic hero. There is nothing monstrous about having philosophical breakdowns. It is scary, but not monstrous. (Light Yagami from Death Note is firmly on the monster side but not because he kills criminals, but because he doesn't care about human life too much, That is why he ends up building a death cult.)<br /> <br /> 2. It is possible to descend into monstrosity by slowly abusing more and more of your power however as I said - It is mid to end game horror. In the early game PCs are relatively pure and usually have little to no intention to violate other people. Which makes the early game devoid of the uneasiness that is present in Vampire. I'm looking for something constant, some kind of looming presence over PCs that eventually makes them crack.<br /> On that note <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar421_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"421\" data-vbnamecard=\"421\">Dataweaver</a> I'm warming up to quiet idea, but I' t sure if making it effectively the effect of psychosis instead of some part of their very nature won't make the situation look like it's not entirely their fault. Hunger and Anger are more disturbing because it feels like it's YOU, like it's your very essence that did the deed. Psychosis comes and goes and it feels more like a brain-fart.<br /> <br /> <br /> Understood - However I have yet to meet a GM that can actually pull this off reliably. Especially with certain people who cannot be unsettled by that kind of horror too much because they either don't understand what they did wrong or understand the predicament so damn well that they learned to accept the outcomes it brings.<br /> It also makes for relatively non-horror early game which is totally killing the atmosphere for some people (For example to me.)<br /> However worst case scenario I can resign from the inner-horror in early game and just make it dark on the outside until the revelation strikes.</div> </div> </div> </div>First of all, as someone involved with a bit of activism, I think you'd need to do a lot better than &quot;goes out to protests&quot; to capture the people who REALLY care about humanity. Maybe it's just a difference of opinion, but I can look back on years of me going out to protests semi-regularly and say that I wasn't doing all that much good for the world when that was where my engagement started and ended. But even if you accept that, your generous estimate is around 33%. That is 1 in 3. So what about the other two? If we wanted to be shitty, we could assume that one of them is &quot;neutral&quot; (in D&amp;D terms), not actively fucking others over but not at all invested in making the world as a whole better, and the other is actively an asshole, making life worse for everyone for their own benefit. But even if we are more generous, you have still just posited a situation where the &quot;good&quot; Mages are the minority, which was all that I was really trying to say.<br /> <br /> Also, a quick caveat: part of the premise of the World of Darkness is that you are taking our world, but marinading it in bloody shit for longer than the span of human history. Statistics from our world should be adjusted with that context in mind.<br /> <br /> I think part of what makes this tricky is that the stuff you *dislike* about Mage, regarding monstrosity being subtle and slow-to-build, are things that many other Mage fans (myself included) would think of as the best features. Having your monstrosity be hardwired into you by virtue of being some kind of non-human Other is kind of low-hanging fruit. It's not hard to add horror to a situation where the player can be forced against their will to commit monstrous crimes by the mechanics of the game. Mage horror is about the most human kinds of Personal Horror precisely because it isn't driven by blatantly non-human impulses. If that is what you want, then it will need to be something that you add to the game, because it just isn't built into the core model.<br /> <br /> Once again giving <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> a nod, it's pretty important to consider that adding Personal Horror elements to the player experience when they actively do not want to engage with it is going to be a Sisyphean task. If the game where an urge to murder people is hardwired into your undead flesh can still be turned into a Superhero-with-Fangs storyline by the players not wanting to engage with the horror of that idea, how would you expect it to be easier with a bunch of reality-warping demigods-in-the-making? Same goes for players that refuse to be horrified when you reveal the horrible implications of their actions; if all of your players choose to be sociopathic monsters who don't care about the lives they ruin in their wake, you're going to have almost as much trouble introducing Personal Horror into a game of Vampire as you would a game of Mage.<br /> <br /> You seem to have an extremely specific idea of what &quot;horror&quot; and &quot;monstrosity&quot; mean, an idea that doesn't even hold up well in the context of the other game-lines. How are Hunger and Rage different from a &quot;psychosis&quot;? It doesn't really make sense to me that you regard one as Personal Horror and the other as a &quot;brain fart.&quot; \u200bAlso, if you're not going to consider any definition of &quot;monstrous&quot; that doesn't involve blatant psychopathy, how exactly *do* you plan on getting players to engage with that, unless they are diving into it with glee and will refuse to be shocked by any horror you throw at them?<br /> <br /> Is a Vampire that desperately laments every person who dies as the result of a Hunger Frenzy a &quot;monster,&quot; or are they &quot;complicated&quot;?<br /> <br /> Is a Werewolf that rips human beings apart because (with some justification) they think that humanity as a species is destroying both the world they live in and the god they worship behaving like a &quot;monster,&quot; or is it &quot;complicated&quot;?<br /> <br /> Most games of VtM struggle walking that knife's edge, and that is exactly what the game was built to do (at least initially). Like I said before, very few players actually want to play Louis from <u>Interview with the Vampire</u>, with a complex internal struggle that slowly gets worn down by their innate monstrosity; they're mostly either going to be aspiring saints or murderhobos, and most of the space in between will be filled by bouts of murderhobo-ery with excuses piled up around them. That's just not something you have the power to change all by yourself as a Storyteller.\u200b<br /> <br /> I also think there is something missing if you can't see something as monstrous when it's done with &quot;good intentions.&quot; If I had the power to brainwash everyone around me, and regularly used that power to warp their beliefs and feelings into whatever I thought was &quot;for their own good,&quot; would you NOT see me as a monster? (It's not letting me tag for some reason, but @Bluecho's point about the &quot;Fascist Urge&quot; is precisely the sort of thing I'm talking about here.)<br /> <br /> If you want to introduce Personal Horror to your players, you're going to have to TEMPT them with it. That's why you have to start small: they have to be willing to make a small exception to their principles before they make a bigger one, and then a bigger one, and a bigger one, all without realizing it's happening, until suddenly they find that they are frogs in a pot of Personal Horror that has slowly been building up to a boil. That's the kind of Personal Horror that Mage is built for, and it's going to take a fair amount of work to beat it into shape if you want something else.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25886-kharnov","name":"Kharnov","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"683"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-04T22:20:30-08:00","id":1530007,"title":"","content":"I wouldn't consider the \u201cFascist Urge\u201d to be horror according to <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28345-sirresnick\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"28345\" data-vbnamecard=\"28345\">SirResnick</a>'s notion of horror \u2014 which is not to say that it's not something to consider including; but it doesn't actually do what's being looked for, at least not in its own. Quiet, on the other hand, <i>does</i>; or at least potentially does. And it <i>does </i>tie in to the Fascist Urge, in that if you've got Denial that blinds you to evidence that you may be wrong, it reinforces a crucial element of the Fascist Urge. Likewise with Madness that <i>creates</i> evidence that you're <i>right</i>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-05T09:00:54-08:00","id":1530033,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1529745-mage-the-ascention-as-personal-horror-game?p=1530007#post1530007\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I wouldn't consider the \u201cFascist Urge\u201d to be horror according to <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28345-sirresnick\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"28345\" data-vbnamecard=\"28345\">SirResnick</a>'s notion of horror \u2014 which is not to say that it's not something to consider including; but it doesn't actually do what's being looked for, at least not in its own. Quiet, on the other hand, <i>does</i>; or at least potentially does. And it <i>does </i>tie in to the Fascist Urge, in that if you've got Denial that blinds you to evidence that you may be wrong, it reinforces a crucial element of the Fascist Urge. Likewise with Madness that <i>creates</i> evidence that you're <i>right</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you're probably correct about that. I'm getting the kind of vibe that you would find in &quot;Animal I Have Become&quot; by Three Days Grace or &quot;Monster&quot; by Skillet. I suppose I'm just kind of trying to push an alternative idea: that maybe it's more scary when you *don't* have the convenient excuse of a Monster Inside to explain the horror of what you have become. There is no Beast, only your own reflection in the abyss staring back at you.<br /> <br /> I've stayed out of the conversation about mechanics because I think that your thoughts on Quiet and what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar758_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"758\" data-vbnamecard=\"758\">Ramnesis</a> said earlier about Paradox are probably the best options if you want to stick to modifying the base mechanics that already exist, or <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> idea about introducing the addiction to Mystery from Awakening 2E.<br /> <br /> Actually, I did just have an idea for how to build a metric ton of Personal Horror into the existence of a Mage and making it a part of their Avatar: have them be a Widderslainte. I haven't done enough reading to know for sure, but I think I recall reading about some Widderslainte that *try* to be better, no matter how inevitable their failure. It might especially make sense if the Nephandi's previous incarnation had undergone a process similar to Senex's apprentice <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Amanda_Jonsson\" target=\"_blank\">Amanda</a>. But those are exceptional situations, not the default.<br /> <br /> If you want the motives of the Avatar to be what pushes your Mages towards Personal Horror, then you probably need to settle what exactly those motives are. It doesn't need to be the same thing for every Avatar, but there probably should be some kind of common theme or concept. For example, maybe every Avatar is on a quest to become God, with what exactly that would look like being unique to every Avatar, and every other Avatar is a rival in that pursuit of apotheosis. Not only do you have an impulse towards general narcissism and megalomania, but you've also built a Highlander-esque source of inherent conflict into the soul of every Mage, which could help to explain their constant problem with <a href=\"https://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/WeAreStrugglingTogether\" target=\"_blank\">We ARE Struggling Together</a>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25886-kharnov","name":"Kharnov","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"683"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-14T21:21:36-08:00","id":1530866,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SirResnick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1529745-mage-the-ascention-as-personal-horror-game?p=1529750#post1529750\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You mean like monkey paw logic ? It often works, but takes ages to actually develop with PCs. On the day-to-day basis being a mage is not too horrifying. Unless you factor in the Big Brother and paranoia that goes with it. I don't want to authoritatively say one thing or the other, so I'll try to convey what I feel.<br /> When I play mage I feel grim gothic atmosphere around me but hopeful and powerful inside me (Which is the opposite of horror)<br /> Then when I play Vampire or Demon I feel that grimness inside me and I can feel the creeping evil that corrupts my actions and thoughts.<br /> <br /> So it is not necessarily bad to be mage, but it just so happens that it often has unforeseen fuck-ups. Which is unsettling when you do it a few times, but to me the theme doesn't hold the atmosphere for long as it is by nature &quot;the conclusion of actions&quot; not the action itself. So the majority of session there is little to be unsettled about, because at heart mages are well meaning to humanity with a few exceptions.<br /> Unlike in Vampire where killing is in the Vampire's nature and reinforced by the kindred community. So you do it and think about it on a daily basis.<br /> That is why I want to give mage community a reason to be horrific not merely stumble over their own power.<br /> <br /> The hubris is a good measure system, but it doesn't feel organic if you assume that this hubris stems only from their humanity.<br /> I think I just don't believe that most people with reality warping superpowers would be automatically possessed by hubris to the point of being inhuman. I do believe that they will often fuck-up in a major way, but that does not make them monsters in my eyes. They would be tragic heroes at best.<br /> I feel like someone is a monster when I see him as completely/partially inhuman and devoid of some or all human qualities like basic empathy.<br /> For example psychopaths are monstrous in my mind despite being Homo Sapiens.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Honestly, i just ignore the part about the average mage being the average joe. Admittedly its a retcon, or at least an extremely cynical interpretation, but you should check the seers of the throne from mage the awakening. Then make the Traditions less bad than, uhm, that, but more bad than your neighbour Jonathan.\\<br /> <br /> Now, i am not suggesting that they be portrayed as &quot;monsters&quot;, but there's a big gap between &quot;monster&quot; and &quot;Joe average&quot; I really do not believe that being given reality warping powers would leave the average person exactly the same. Actually, forget about reality warping. Focus on &quot;powers&quot;. Whether power corrupts or reveals what was hidden within is a moot point<br /> Whether or not this counts as personal horror depends on what you mean by that. If we interpret that as &quot;feel shit on the inside&quot; then as <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar421_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"421\" data-vbnamecard=\"421\">Dataweaver</a> said quite goes a long way towards making you feel like a monster on the inside. The Ultimate POWER merely helps. For instance, maybe you feel shit because of the quiet. You gained it by saving your wife. The bitch put herself in danger and now its you who pay the price. So you &quot;educate&quot; her.(not talking about rape btw) Not that she can complain, because you have ultimate power. Then you keep knowing that you are right. Its so clear, such clarity of vision! And a small part knows you are insane and is screaming for the inside for *help* All forms of quiet are useful. <br /> <br /> I find that quiet is a rather underappreciated mechanic in spite of the lore establishing it as such. Maybe its cause i also play Ars Magica but i love it","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-19T19:32:03-08:00","id":1531168,"title":"","content":"Mages my not have humanity, but they can get paradox and quiet, which can make them worse than a zero-humanity mortal. <br /> <br /> I did create a Mage-game, back in the day, based off of Hexen, in that, mages controlled a mountain-kingdom, and, ruthlessly guarded their powers. They had the military hunting down other awakened mages in a totalitarian-society.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"738"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}